</user_query>"}]},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Great question! You have two logical options:

### 1. **Use hasNotificationPermission to Make the Function More Efficient**

You can use the hasNotificationPermission parameter to avoid launching the permission request and showing dialogs if permission is already granted. This makes the UI more efficient and avoids unnecessary requests/interactions.

**How to use it:** 
- At the start of your composable, check if hasNotificationPermission is true.
- If true, you can simply return early (do nothing or just call onPermissionResult(true)), meaning you don't render or interact with any of the permission logic/UI.
- Only execute the rest of the permission logic if hasNotificationPermission is false.

**Example snippet:**
```kotlin
@Composable
fun NotificationPermissionHandler(
 showPermissionDialog: Boolean,
 hasNotificationPermission: Boolean,
 onPermissionResult: (Boolean) -> Unit,
 onDismissDialog: () -> Unit,
) {
 if (hasNotificationPermission) {
 // Optionally notify the caller immediately
 onPermissionResult(true)
 return
 }
 // ... rest of the existing code ...
}
```

### 2. **Remove hasNotificationPermission if Not Needed**

If you prefer to keep all permission-checking logic internal (e.g., using NotificationPermissionUtils yourself) and never use hasNotificationPermission from outside, just remove the parameter—having unused parameters is confusing and can result in bugs.

---

### **Recommended Approach**
If permission information is available outside this composable (and so passed in), option 1 is best! 
If not, and you want this composable to manage checking itself, use option 2.

Let me know which approach you prefer and if you'd like help modifying the code accordingly!"}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<user_query>
